associated with worse psychological outcomes.", "keywords": ["highly significant relationship", "substantially elevated rates", "significantly associated"], "direction": "significant", "effect_strength": "strong", "hedging": false, "claim": "Excessive social media engagement is significantly associated with worse psychological outcomes, with participants in the highest screen time quartile showing substantially elevated rates of moderate to severe depressive symptoms."}], "primary_direction": "significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["results"]}, {"test_type": "Q", "statistic_value": 48.45, "df1": 24.0, "df2": null, "reported_p": 0.54, "p_equality": "=", "two_tailed": true, "raw_text": "Q(24) = 48.45, p = 0.54", "textual_interpretation": "treatment efficacy remained relatively consistent across different time points and did not vary significantly across individual sessions", "interpretation_direction": "not_significant", "consistent": true, "notes": "Meta-analysis using Cochran's Q statistic in a subsample of 68 participants receiving cognitive-behavioral intervention for social anxiety", "interpretations": [{"section": "results", "sentence": "The Q-test revealed no significant heterogeneity in session-to-session effect sizes, Q(24) = 48.45, p = 0.54, suggesting that treatment efficacy remained relatively consistent across different time points and did not vary significantly across individual sessions. This homogeneity in treatment response indicates that the intervention produced stable therapeutic benefits throughout the intervention period, without evidence of differential effectiveness at particular stages of treatment.", "keywords": ["no significant heterogeneity", "remained relatively consistent", "did not vary significantly", "homogeneity", "stable therapeutic benefits", "without evidence of differential effectiveness"], "direction": "not_significant", "effect_strength": "none", "hedging": false, "claim": "The Q-test revealed no significant heterogeneity in session-to-session effect sizes, suggesting that treatment efficacy remained relatively consistent across different time points and individual sessions, indicating stable therapeutic benefits."}], "primary_direction": "not_significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["results"]}], "verified_tests": [{"test_type": "chi", "statistic_value": 15.88, "df1": 11.0, "df2": null, "reported_p": 0.16, "p_equality": "=", "two_tailed": true, "raw_text": "χ²(11) = 15.88, p = 0.16", "textual_interpretation": "platform preference did not differ significantly between male and female participants", "interpretation_direction": "not_significant", "consistent": true, "notes": "Initial chi-square analysis examining the distribution of social media platform preferences across the sample", "interpretations": [{"section": "results", "sentence": "Initial chi-square analysis examining the distribution of social media platform preferences (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, Snapchat, Twitter, YouTube, Reddit, Discord, BeReal, Mastodon, Threads, and Bluesky) across the sample revealed no significant association with gender, χ²(11) = 15.88, p = 0.16, indicating that platform preference did not differ significantly between male and female participants.", "keywords": ["no significant association", "did not differ significantly"], "direction": "not_significant", "effect_strength": "none", "hedging": false, "claim": "Platform preference did not differ significantly between male and female participants."}], "primary_direction": "not_significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["results"], "computed_p": 0.1456446317, "p_consistency": "marginal", "interpretation_consistency": "consistent"}, {"test_type": "chi", "statistic_value": 29.32, "df1": 3.0, "df2": null, "reported_p": 0.001, "p_equality": "<", "two_tailed": true, "raw_text": "χ²(3) = 29.32, p < 0.001", "textual_interpretation":